**Dual-write.** Legacy `pg-orders-1` still serves every read and write. Each write also lands in `orders_outbox` in the same transaction, and `outbox-relay` replays it to orders-v2. A nightly backfill copies the historical orders. Nothing reads orders-v2 yet, so customers can't see its errors. The phase ends when four things hold:

- the backfill is complete;
- a full-table checksum matches;
- `order-diff` mismatches stay under 0.01% for 3 consecutive days;
- relay lag p99 stays under 2 seconds for the same 3 days.

**Cutover.** `orders.read_from_v2` moves reads to orders-v2 in steps of 1%, 10%, 50% and 100%, one step a day. Writes still go to both stores during the ramp. Reads then stay at 100% for a 48-hour soak. Until the soak ends, rolling back just means setting that flag to 0. Turning off `orders.write_legacy` is one-way: legacy stops getting writes and nothing syncs back to it. That's why this step needs sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director.

The write stop is scheduled for 2026-11-30, the day before the code freeze. If it slips, dual-write keeps running until January.

**Dual-write.** Legacy still serves all reads and takes all writes. Each write also commits an `orders_outbox` row in the same transaction, and `outbox-relay` replays those rows to orders-v2 in commit order. `backfill_orders_v2` copies historical orders each night. Nothing reads from orders-v2 yet. The phase passes when four checks hold:

- the backfill is complete;
- the `order-diff` mismatch rate stays under 0.01% for 3 days;
- relay lag p99 stays under 2 seconds for the same 3 days;
- a full-table checksum comparison matches.

**Cutover.** `orders.read_from_v2` ramps reads to orders-v2 at 1%, 10%, 50% and 100%, one step a day. Writes still go to both stores during the ramp, which is followed by a 48-hour soak at 100%. Until the soak ends, rollback is setting the flag back to 0. Turning off `orders.write_legacy` can't be undone because nothing syncs back to legacy, so that step needs sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director. After that, legacy stays read-only for 30 days and is then decommissioned.

The timeline has no slack. The four ramp steps fill 2026-11-24 to 11-27 exactly, so holding any step pushes the write stop past 11-30 into the code freeze. Dual-write would then run until January.

**Shadow stage (2026-11-10 to 2026-11-17).** v7 scores every search request alongside v6, but v6 still serves every result page, so no user sees v7. The `rank-shadow` sidecar logs v7's top 10 next to v6's, and a nightly job summarizes it in `ranker_shadow_daily`. The stage runs at least 7 days and must include a weekend. To pass, every day must show:

- p99 v7 latency of 45 ms or less;
- errors at or under 0.1%;
- top-10 overlap with v6 between 55% and 85%;
- no feature null in more than 2% of requests.

The search lead and the SRE on call then sign off. Overlap above 85% does not count as a pass. It stops the rollout for a modelling review, because v7 would be too close to v6 for an A/B test to tell them apart.

The document places sign-off at the "Thursday ranking review," but the timeline schedules the review for 2026-11-20, which is a Friday.

**Live A/B stage (2026-11-24 to 2026-12-08).** 10% of logged-in users get v7, and another 10% stay on v6 as a control. Users are assigned by hashed `user_id`, so each one stays in the same group across devices. The primary metric is successful-search rate, with a baseline of 41.2%. v7 passes if that rate improves by at least 1.0% relative, read once at day 14 and not before. The guardrails are zero-result rate, page latency and revenue. The test can stop early for harm, but never for success.

The revenue guardrail can't actually be tested. At this sample size the test only detects a revenue drop of about 4%, so a flat revenue reading doesn't show v7 is safe. Finance treats revenue as monitored rather than gated, and plans a separate revenue holdout after launch.

**`PAYMENTS_SETTLEMENT_MODE`** is the configuration value that controls how `chargeOrder()` writes ledger rows. In `legacy` mode it writes one row per order; in `split` mode it writes two. Release `v412` changed it from `legacy` to `split`. The release job published that config before replacing any instances, and `v411`'s `assertSettlementMode()` throws on values it doesn't recognise, so every checkout on the `v411` instances failed. That was the trigger at 14:02.

**Migration `0042_add_settlement_currency`** is the second step of the `v412` release job, and it ran at 14:05. In one transaction it:

```sql
ALTER TABLE ledger_entries ADD COLUMN settlement_currency char(3);
UPDATE ledger_entries SET settlement_currency = 'USD';
ALTER TABLE ledger_entries ALTER COLUMN settlement_currency SET NOT NULL;
```

The column has no default and is `NOT NULL`, so every insert has to supply it. `v411` code never writes that column. As a result, reverting the config to `legacy` and rolling back to `v411` both still failed, now on a not-null violation instead of the mode assertion. The fix was to roll forward to `v412`, which writes the column in both modes.
